<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>First principles: differentiating the reciprocal k/x (INNODEMS)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>You have found derivatives of polynomials from first principles by <em>expanding</em> and cancelling \(h\). Let's see how we can find the derivative of \[ f(x) = {@fdisp@} \] at the point \( x = {@a@} \).</p>
<p><strong>Explore first.</strong> The graph plots the difference quotient
\[ q(h) = \frac{f({@a@}+h) - f({@a@})}{h} = \frac{\ \dfrac{ {@k@} }{x+h} - \dfrac{ {@k@} }{x}\ }{h}. \]
against \(h\). Drag the blue point along the curve toward \( h = 0 \) and watch the read-out. The small open circle marks \( h = 0 \), where \( q(h) \) is <em>not defined</em>.</p>
[[jsxgraph width='560px' height='440px']]
JXG.Options.text.useMathJax = true;
var k = {#k#}, a = {#a#};
var hmin = {#float(hmin)#}, hmax = {#float(hmax)#};
var xL = {#float(xL)#}, xR = {#float(xR)#}, yT = {#float(yT)#}, yB = {#float(yB)#};
var hole = {#float(holef)#};
var g = function (h) { return -k / (a * (a + h)); };

var board = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, {
    boundingbox: [xL, yT, xR, yB],
    keepaspectratio: false,
    axis: true,
    showCopyright: false,
    pan: {enabled: false},
});

// axis labels
board.create('text', [xR - 0.15, 0, 'h'],
    {anchorX: 'right', anchorY: 'bottom', fontSize: 15, color: '#555', fixed: true});
board.create('text', [xL + 0.08, yT - 0.05, 'q(h)'],
    {anchorX: 'left', anchorY: 'top', fontSize: 15, color: '#555', fixed: true});

// the difference-quotient curve
var curve = board.create('functiongraph', [g, hmin, hmax],
    {strokeColor: '#0055bb', strokeWidth: 2.5});

// faint dashed line at the limiting level (value deliberately NOT labelled)
board.create('line', [[xL, hole], [xR, hole]],
    {straightFirst: true, straightLast: true, strokeColor: '#c9c9c9',
     strokeWidth: 1, dash: 2, fixed: true, highlight: false});

// the removed point at h=0, drawn as an open circle
board.create('point', [0, hole],
    {name: '', size: 4, strokeColor: '#c62828', fillColor: '#ffffff',
     strokeWidth: 2, fixed: true, highlight: false});
board.create('text', [0.14, hole, 'undefined at \\(h=0\\)'],
    {anchorX: 'left', anchorY: 'middle', fontSize: 13, color: '#c62828', fixed: true});

// draggable point on the curve; the student slides it toward h=0
var G = board.create('glider', [hmax * 0.8, g(hmax * 0.8), curve],
    {name: '', size: 5, strokeColor: '#1565c0', fillColor: '#1565c0'});

// live read-out; near h=0 the quotient is reported as undefined (0/0)
board.create('text', [
    function () { return board.getBoundingBox()[0] + 0.18; },
    function () { return board.getBoundingBox()[1] - 0.18; },
    function () {
        var h = G.X(), q = G.Y();
        var qstr = (Math.abs(h) < 0.015) ? 'undefined (0/0)' : q.toFixed(3);
        return '<div style="background:rgba(255,255,255,0.92);padding:6px 10px;'
            + 'border-radius:5px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;color:#222">'
            + 'h = ' + h.toFixed(3) + '<br>'
            + 'q(h) = ' + qstr + '</div>';
    }
], {useHTML: true, anchorX: 'left', anchorY: 'top', fixed: true});
[[/jsxgraph]]
<div>As \( h \to 0 \), the value of \( q(h) \) approaches (give your estimate to \(2\) decimal places): \( q(h) \to \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<div>Why can you <em>not</em> find the gradient by simply putting \( h = 0 \) into \( q(h) \)? [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>
<p><strong>Now use algebra</strong>, for a general \( x \). Combine the numerator over the common denominator \( x(x+h) \), then cancel the factor of \( h \) to get:</p>
<div>\( q(h) = \) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>
<div>Take the limit as \( h \to 0 \): \( f'(x) = \) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>
<div>Hence the exact gradient at \( x = {@a@} \) is \( f'({@a@}) = \) [[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]]</div>
<p>Compare this exact value with the estimate you read from the graph.</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>For \( f(x) = {@fdisp@} \), form the difference quotient and combine over the common denominator \( x(x+h) \):</p>
\[ \frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} = \frac{\dfrac{ {@k@} }{x+h} - \dfrac{ {@k@} }{x}}{h} = \frac{ {@k@}\bigl(x-(x+h)\bigr)}{h\,x(x+h)} = \frac{ {@negk@}\,h}{h\,x(x+h)} = \frac{ {@negk@} }{x(x+h)}. \]
<p>The factor of \( h \) cancels &mdash; which is exactly why you cannot put \( h = 0 \) at the start: before cancelling, that gives \( \tfrac{0}{0} \), an indeterminate form. Now let \( h \to 0 \), so \( x+h \to x \):</p>
\[ f'(x) = \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{ {@negk@} }{x(x+h)} = \frac{ {@negk@} }{x\cdot x} = {@fpx@}. \]
<p>At \( x = {@a@} \): \( f'({@a@}) = {@fpa@} \approx {@dispdp(holef,3)@} \), which matches the value the graph was closing in on.</p>
<p>Notice \( f'(x) = {@fpx@} = {@negk@}\,x^{-2} \). This is the same result the <strong>power rule</strong> gives with \( n = -1 \), which you meet in the next section.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
